    </figure>

    <!-- ============ QUICK ANSWER ============ -->
    <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-red-50 to-orange-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-900 mb-4">⚡ Quick Answer</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-red-800 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Mutianyu</strong> is the best all-round choice for most first-time visitors — fully restored and dramatic, with a cable car up and a toboggan down, at a fraction of Badaling's crowds. <strong>Badaling</strong> is the definitive one — the first section opened to tourists, the stretch visited by heads of state, and the most monumental of the restored stretches, climbing to the 888 m North Eighth Tower; it's also by far the busiest. <strong>Jinshanling</strong> is the hiker's and photographer's pick — quieter and less rebuilt, with 67 watchtowers and a pre-dawn opening for the sea of clouds. <strong>Simatai</strong> is the atmospheric one — year-round night tours, with Gubei Water Town right at its foot. <strong>Huanghuacheng</strong> is the oddity — the only section where the wall drops into open water, with boats and a glass bridge. <strong>Juyongguan</strong> is the closest and quietest pass — a fortress loop rather than a ridge climb, and the best half-day or winter bet.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ IN THIS GUIDE ============ -->
    <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 In This Guide</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li><a href="#which-section" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🤔 Which Section Should You Visit?</a></li>
